#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Run one Claude Code turn for a Kanban card in its Hermes-owned worktree.

Prints exactly one JSON line on stdout. The Hermes worker that calls this
script relays the line to the board; this process is fenced from board
mutations, so it never touches Kanban itself.

A card on the Triage board triages its issue: Claude gets the triage rules in
place of the implementation rules, and its settings refuse every publishing
command. Any other card starts only when its Linear issue carries
`ready-for-agent`.

Claude Code starts with the allowlisted environment in CHILD_ENV_NAMES and
CHILD_ENV_PREFIXES. The run's Linear token reaches it only through the per-run
settings file, mode 0600, as LINEAR_API_KEY, which the commands Claude runs
also get on purpose: LINEAR_RULE tells Claude to use it. Claude Code removes that file through a SessionStart
hook as soon as it has read it. claude-session also removes it before each run
and when it ends, whether it returns or stops on SIGTERM, SIGINT, or SIGHUP.
The proxy token and the token of each MCP server in claude_session.mcp_servers
never enter Claude Code's environment: the settings point apiKeyHelper at
proxy-token, each such server's headersHelper is mcp-headers, and both ask the
gateway's broker.

--mcp-status --repo <full_name> starts Claude Code once in the repository's
main checkout with a run's environment, settings, and MCP config, reads its
init event, and prints each MCP server's status. It never reaches the model.
It disables all hooks, so the repository's own hooks never run.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import fcntl
import json
import os
import re
